      "id": 1,
      "text": "North College Hill was created in 1916 when three east‑Hamilton subdivisions merged, and it was named after the Farmers’ College founded in 1846.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_College_Hill%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Cary Cottage, built in 1832, now stands on the Clovernook Center campus and is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_College_Hill%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1903 the Clovernook Center for the Blind and Visually Impaired opened on Cary Cottage grounds, founded by sisters Florence and Georgia Trader.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_College_Hill%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "John Cleves Symmes surveyed the land in 1787, securing the Symmes Purchase that later became the area of North College Hill.",
